      "text": "This is a significantly harder problem than the other problems solved so far, so I'll outline the solution in steps.\n\nFirst, note that if $x=y,$ then the equation becomes\n$\\boxed{2^{2x(2x + 1)}} = 1,$\nwhich holds if and only if $x=0.$ Hence, we need only consider the case where $x \\neq y.$\n\nAlso, for nonzero values of $x, y,$ the left side of the equation is strictly increasing. In particular, for $x \\neq y,$ there exist values of $x, y$ which satisfies the equation.\n\nClaim. If $x=0,$ then $y$ can be any real number.\nProof. Clearly, $x=0$ does satisfy the equation. So we only need to show that for any $y,$ there exists a value of $x$ such that the desired equation holds. In other words, we must show that the inequality $\\dfrac{1}{4} < 2^{x(2x + 1)} < 1$ holds if and only if $x \\neq 0.$ Since $2^{x(2x + 1)} > \\dfrac{1}{4}$ for all real numbers $x$ by the AM-GM inequality, it suffices to prove that the inequality $\\dfrac{1}{4} < 2^{x(2x + 1)} < 1$ holds for all real numbers $x,$ except $x=0.$\n\nWe have\n\\[\\dfrac{1}{4} < 2^{x(2x + 1)} < 1 \\Leftrightarrow \\log \\frac{1}{4} < \\log 2^{x(2x + 1)} < 0 \\Leftrightarrow -\\log 4 < x(2x + 1) < 0,\\] which holds if and only if $x=0$ is the only possible solution, which yields the desired result.\n\nClaim. There does not exist any $(x, y)$ with $x \\neq 0, y \\neq 0$ and $xy=0$ such that\n\\[2^{2x(2x + 1)} = 1.\\]\n\nProof. Note that\n\\[2^{2x (2x + 1)} = \\left( 2^{2x} \\right)\\left ( 2^{2x}\\right)> 2^{2x}\\]\nfor all values of $x.$ Hence, $\\left( 2^{2x} \\right)\\left ( 2^{2x}\\right) = 1$ can only occur if either $2^{2x} = 2^{2x}$ or $2^{2x} = 2^{0}.$ But in neither case is $2^{2x}=2^{2x}.$\n\nClaim. There does not exist any $(x, y)$ with $x \\neq 0, y \\neq 0$ and $x=0$ such that\n\\[\\left( 2^{2x} \\right) \\left( 2^{2x}\\right) = 1.\\]\n\nProof. Note that $2^{2x}$ cannot be equal to $2^{2x}$ since $2^{2x}$ is just one number. So the desired solution is also impossible.\nThus, $\\boxed{2}$ ordered pairs $(x,y)$ (which correspond to $y=0$ or $xy=0)$ satisfy the original equation.",
