      "text": "We begin by writing both terms of the equation as a power of $2^{y}$. Then we substitute $x = -y$ and notice that we must have $2\\sqrt{|xy|}$ in the exponent. Finally, note that if $x = 0$ then either $y$ or $-y$ is zero, and if $x \\notin \\{0,-1,1\\}$ then $xy \\notin \\{0,-1,1\\}\\cup(-1,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$, meaning that $2|xy|=0$, therefore\n\\[((2^{|x|})^{xy})+(\\frac{1}{16})^{2xy}=1\\implies2|x|+2^{-2xy}=1\\]\nFrom here we see that $-7\\leq x\\leq7$, since $0<2^{-2xy}<1$. Thus, we may wish to investigate $4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}\\geq1\\geq4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},-x},4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},-2x}$ and find that\n\\[4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}\\geq1\\iff|2x/3|+4^{-x/3} \\geq 1,\\\\ 4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}\\geq4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},-2x}\\iff|2x/3| + 4^{-x/3} \\geq 4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},-2x}\\iff-2x+4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}\\\\\\leq 3, \\quad \\frac {|2x/3|}{4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}} \\leq 1,\\,-2=4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}\\leq \\frac {3}{|-2x|}\\implies4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}-\\frac{x}{2}=1>\\frac {3}{2}\\implies4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}>\\frac {7}{2},\\,|x|-4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}>-2\\implies|2x/3|-8^{-x/3} >0 \\implies\\frac {|2x/3|}{4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}} =1< \\frac {3}{2}\\implies4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x}-\\frac{x}{2}=1<\\frac {-2}{3}\\implies4^{|2x/3|,4^{-x/3},x} <3\\left(\\frac {1}{\\sqrt 2}\\right)^{-3} \\implies4^{2x/3,4^{-x/3},-x}=\\left|4^{-2x/3,4^{2x/3},x}\\right|=1\\]\nThus, the set of points that also satisfies\n\\[4^{2x/3,4^{-x/3},-x}=\\left|4^{-2x/3,4^{2x/3},x}\\right|=1\\] must have $x$ points in it, and then there are $|7x|+1$ points in total.\n\nNow, we fix $-1$, $0$, and $1$ in the domain and for each of those $3$ values we plug into the equation. That's $31$ solutions. But where we take the domain to be $R$ then there are $77$ points by themselves, and if we apply symmetry to it, there will be $495$ points which entirely obey the given equation. So, there are $\\boxed{566}$ ordered pairs that completely satisfy the equation. The answer is 566",
